WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.870
Question: Which country would you say is the
US’s biggest ally?

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:14.870
Is it Canada?

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:15.870
Maybe the UK?

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:16.950
And what about Israel?

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.470
It is not an easy question to answer and it
definitely depends on who you ask…

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:26.440
However, there is a country which has improved
its alliance with the US a great deal in the

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:27.440
last few years.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.880
It has improved enough that it is, for Republicans,
the best friend of the country, its biggest

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.590
ally, more so than any other country.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:39.730
And no, it is not Canada or the UK, it is
Australia.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.870
The country of surf, kangaroos and the Sydney
Opera House.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:44.969
Surprised?

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:49.350
Well, you shouldn´t be...

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.429
Australia is, dear viewer, the only country
in the world to have fought with the US in

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:55.010
all their big wars:

00:00:55.010 --> 00:01:00.969
WWI, WWII, Korea, Vietnam, the Gulf War, Afghanistan,
Iraq and, also know, in the operations against

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:01.969
ISIS.

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:07.170
For the Pentagon, we could almost say Australia
is to the USA what the score is to a movie.

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:12.000
It at first, might go unnoticed, but remove
that music, and you can see that it’s really

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.130
something that is missed.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:19.530
On the 28th January 2017, and despite this
old alliance, Donald Trump decided to hang

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.399
up the phone on Malcolm Turnbull, Australia´s
Primer Minister.

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:27.570
Not just that, before he told him to get lost
and hung up on him, Trump accused him of trying

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.009
to export terrorists to the United States!

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:34.689
And this was all about a slight disagreement
that these two countries had over 1250 refugees...

00:01:34.689 --> 00:01:39.180
but, you know, that’s a story we’re going
to get to another day…

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.470
Yes, brief diplomatic tussle might not seem
like that much in the grand scheme of things,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.200
but it has had a lot of consequences.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.390
Many Australians are left wondering if it
isn't time to start to cooling off the relationship

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:55.359
with the US just a little.

00:01:55.359 --> 00:02:01.219
So, well, this is something we started wondering
as well…

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:04.920
It raises questions like: How important is
Australia for the United States?

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.970
And was it really sensible for Trump to hang
up the phone on his greatest ally?

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.830
And what kind of consequences is this going
to have for him?

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:17.140
Well, let’s take a look shall we?

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.140
TIME FOR ASIA

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:23.030
It’s probably not news to you... especially
if you have been following this channel for

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.189
a while: The world has changed and both the
political and economic power centres are moving

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:29.189
quickly to Asia.

00:02:29.189 --> 00:02:33.420
By 2020 it is expected that more than half
of the world’s economy and population will

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.900
be located in this continent.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.700
And China is emerging as a new superpower.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.720
And that’s a fact.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.480
Up until now, a map of the world has looked
pretty close to this one...

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:52.730
&nbsp;Well, from now on we’re going to see something
closer and closer to this one.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:57.580
As you can see Europe is leaving the centre
of the world, and is taking up a position

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:58.580
in the corner.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:06.590
Now this is just a fun comparison, but we
really can't deny that the world is changing.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:11.140
The 21st Century is going to be Asia’s century...

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.630
Something we’ve discuss a bunch here at
VisualPolitik.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:17.959
That is why former US president Barack Obama
decided to bet strongly on Asia and made this

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:21.299
continent his top priority for American diplomacy.

00:03:21.299 --> 00:03:23.459
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
It was under these circumstances that Australia

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:25.420
become such a valuable friend.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.420
But…

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.349
how close is this friendship between the US
and Australia really?

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:32.470
Well, let’s take a look.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:35.450
A BIG ALLIANCE

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:43.380
In November 2011, Barack Obama and Australia's
Prime Minister at the time, Julia Gillard,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:48.020
signed a military agreement which meant a
new era for military cooperation between these

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.069
two countries.

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:54.379
We could almost say that this new treaty started
a military friendship between the US and Australia.

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:59.840
In fact, let’s take a look at the description
the New York Times wrote about the agreement:

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.549
“The agreement with Australia amounts to
the first long-term expansion of the American

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:08.090
military’s presence in the Pacific since
the end of the Vietnam War”.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:09.090
New York Times.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:10.090
http://www.nytimes.com/2011/11/17/world/asia/obama-and-gillard-expand-us-australia-military-ties.html

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.090
But…what did this agreement really mean?

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:15.219
Well, for starters, thanks to the treaty,
the US can deploy a fighting force of up to

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:17.739
2,500 soldiers of the in Australia…

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:21.530
These troops are from the Marine Corps and
they are able to &nbsp;quickly deploy in the event

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:22.530
of any conflict.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:23.530
&nbsp;

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.280
But don´t think this is the end of things…

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.930
Since then, the collaboration between these
countries has grown and grown...

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:31.450
it has grown enough to become an essential
relationship for Washington.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:35.860
It’s not just the Marines, the United States
wants to make Australia the key home base

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.760
for their activities in both the Pacific and
Indian Ocean.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:47.340
So now you are probably getting the idea - the
US want to have access to the landing strips

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.840
situated in the north of the country, and
they want to display bombers B-2 and B-52

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:52.900
bombers from Australian soil.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:57.320
They also want to set up a large naval base
near Perth for their enormous fleet of nuclear

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:06.580
aircraft carriers and nuclear submarines.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:07.610
And there is more…

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:11.319
the United States is also trying to locate
air monitoring centres and missile launch

00:05:11.319 --> 00:05:12.940
warning facilities there too.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:17.510
They even want to rent part of the Coco Islands
in the Indian Ocean which are under Australian

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:18.510
sovereignty.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:19.870
So, you might now be thinking?

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:20.870
What do you mean?

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:24.830
Why would the United States want to rent some
remote islands in the Indian Ocean?

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:29.990
Well, the answer is clear: the US wants to
locate their long-range surveillance aircrafts

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.300
and armed drones on the Coco Islands...

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:36.350
They also want to use this archipelago as
another deployment centre for their bombers

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:37.690
in case of war.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:39.979
But wait, because there is still more...

00:05:39.979 --> 00:05:45.060
The United States wants to improve the integration
between their army and the Australian military,

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.180
so that the two forces could work together
whenever needed.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.740
Now, if you don’t exactly understand what
the results of that could be, well consider

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:53.740
this…

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:57.699
In the last few years, Australia has carried
out a significant renovation of their military

00:05:57.699 --> 00:05:58.699
equipment.

00:05:58.699 --> 00:06:03.280
We are talking about purchase contracts worth
billions and billions of dollars and, among

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.870
those, there is quite a lot of American material.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:10.819
Take the controversial F35 as an example:
Australia has committed to buy 72 units…

00:06:10.819 --> 00:06:13.000
which is going to cost $19 billion.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.360
That is a serious amount of cash…

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.600
And we are talking about a country of little
more than 24 million inhabitants.

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.500
So per person, it’s really a lot of money…

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:29.550
Alright, so now you’ve got to be wondering,
”Why Australia?”

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:30.550
Right?

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:31.639
Well, for several reasons:

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:36.069
First, because both countries are culturally
similar and, as we said before, they have

00:06:36.069 --> 00:06:37.580
fought together many times.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:42.410
Second, because Australia’s geographical
position is almost perfect: it’s situated

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:47.009
between the Indian and Pacific oceans, and
it is close enough to the “hot spots”

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:51.060
in Asia that the US could carry out attacks
and deploy troops there quickly.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.139
And, last but not least, another crucial factor:

00:06:54.139 --> 00:06:58.199
Australia is an important energy, raw material
and food supplier to China.

00:06:58.199 --> 00:07:02.740
So the Pentagon’s believes that, in the
event of a conflict or tough tensions between

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.530
the two countries, it would be convenient
to have control over Australia.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.060
And, well, what about the other side?

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.229
What do the Australians want?

00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:14.300
They basically understand that the US can
be a sort of insurance policy...

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:18.960
a way to ensure their own security.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:27.129
However, there are some voices who disagree,
and these voices seem to be growing louder.

00:07:27.129 --> 00:07:31.990
And further, Trump hasn't helped with his
whole hanging up the phone situation…

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:37.449
THE CHINESE ALTERNATIVE

00:07:37.449 --> 00:07:42.400
China is, by far, the largest trade partner
for Australia: it is the first destination

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:46.509
for Australia’s exports, the country of
origin for many of the immigrants coming to

00:07:46.509 --> 00:07:50.770
Australia and, besides, there are more and
more Chinese people who visit the country

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.590
every year, or who have decided to study there.

00:07:53.590 --> 00:08:00.310
Anyway, the Chinese mean a lot of money for
the Australians, and that has it’s consequences.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:05.300
“A 2016 survey conducted by the public policy
group Lowy Institute asked respondents to

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.870
identify the country that was more important
to Australia; 43 percent chose the United

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:12.130
States, and 43 percent China.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:17.350
In 2014, 48 percent had answered the United
States, and only 37 percent had chosen China”.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:18.560
New York Times
https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/world/australia/donald-trump-malcolm-turnbull-refugees.html

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.419
And, look, under 45 year-olds clearly opted
for China and almost half of the country’s

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:27.710
population believe their country should keep
some distance from a Trump’s America.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:31.759
Paul Keating, an important Australian ex-prime
minister, for example, is one of the personalities

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:36.430
who thinks it would be better to try to improve
relations with China, and let relations with

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.640
the United States cool down a bit.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:39.640
&nbsp;

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.760
And pay particular attention to how he explains
his position...

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.980
“This society of ours is a better society
than the United States.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.670
It’s more even, it’s more fair.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:52.000
We’ve had a 50 percent increase in real
incomes in the last 20 years.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.200
Median America has had zero, zero.”

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:55.200
Paul Keating

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.200
https://www.nytimes.com/2016/12/16/opinion/dear-america-why-did-you-let-us-down.html

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:58.480
For now, Australia has made some kind gestures
towards the Asian giant:

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:02.780
As an example (and by the way Washington didn't
like this at all) the country decided to participate

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:07.860
in the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank,
an institution lead by China whose aim is

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.330
to increase China's influence in the country.

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:14.500
The Australian government itself has made
it clear that China is, above all, a source

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.270
for opportunities for the country.

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:25.540
Luckily for Trump, there are still many bonds
protecting the alliance he has with Australia.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:31.450
AMERICAN CULTURE AND FEAR OF THE CHINESE DRAGON

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:36.150
Australia and the US are politically, culturally
and economically similar countries.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:40.080
They are so similar that, at an international
level, that it could be argued that Australia

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.870
is nothing more than an extension of the American
lifestyle in Asia.

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:48.300
And the truth is, Australians are still very
proud to be considered that way.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:53.461
“Whatever happens, it will not dull my affection
for the American people and the best of American

00:09:53.461 --> 00:09:54.461
culture.

00:09:54.461 --> 00:09:57.590
I was not raised in America, but I was raised
in the American century.

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:00.140
I am not yet ready to say goodbye”.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:01.140
Lisa Pryor.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:05.470
Furthermore, the United States is still the
largest investor in Australia, an investment

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:08.640
that exceeds $500 billion.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:14.160
Also, the fact that China is still a dictatorship
is making Australia suspicious, many simply

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.930
don't trust Beijing’s intentions.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:20.700
In fact, the government has stopped many Chinese
investment operations that intended to buy

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:21.910
land in Australia.

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:26.650
So, we can say that it all indicates the alliance
with the US will continue, but…

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:30.141
Australians are very practical people, and
they have made it clear they don’t want

00:10:30.141 --> 00:10:35.180
to be affected by a future hypothetical war
of power between China and the United States.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.551
So, that’s what we think here at Visual
Politik, now we’d like to know: what do

00:10:38.551 --> 00:10:39.551
you think?

00:10:39.551 --> 00:10:42.750
Do you think the United States will (despite
Trump) carry out all the projects they had

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:44.040
planned in Australia?

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.700
Or do you think China achieve more and more
influence?

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:51.350
And, most importantly, how would you feel
if Trump had hung up the phone on your country's

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.350
leader!?

